dom","H312":"another","H3808":"not","H3240":"to rest","H6231":"to oppress","H3198":"to rebuke","H5921":"upon","H4428":"king","H408":"not","H5060":"to touch","H4899":"anointed","H5030":"prophet","H7489":"be evil","H1319":"to bear tidings","H3444":"salvation","H5608":"to recount","H3519":"glory","H3588":"for","H1419":"large","H3966":"much","H3372":"to fear","H457":"idol","H8064":"heaven","H1935":"splendor","H1926":"glory","H2304":"joy","H4725":"place","H3051":"to give","H4940":"family","H5375":"raise","H4503":"offering","H7812":"to bow","H1927":"adornment","H2342":"tremble","H637":"also","H3559":"prepare","H8398":"world","H1077":"not","H4131":"to shake","H8056":"glad","H1523":"to rejoice","H4427":"to reign","H7481":"to thunder","H3220":"sea","H4393":"fullness","H5970":"to rejoice","H7704":"Sirion","H7442":"to overcome","H6086":"wood","H3293":"wood","H8199":"to judge","H2896":"pleasant","H2617":"kindness","H3467":"to save","H3468":"salvation","H6908":"to gather","H5337":"to rescue","H7623":"to soothe","H8416":"praise","H543":"amen","H5800":"forsake","H8033":"there","H8346":"sixty","H8083":"eight","H3038":"Jeduthun","H2621":"Hosah","H7778":"gatekeeper","H6659":"Zadok","H4908":"tabernacle","H1116":"high place","H1391":"Gibeon","H4196":"altar","H1242":"morning","H6153":"evening","H3789":"to write","H8451":"instruction","H5973":"with","H1968":"Heman","H7605":"remnant","H1305":"to purify","H5344":"to pierce","H7892":"song","H8179":"gate","H3212":"to walk","H1004":"place","H5437":"turn"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Lord, wishing them all felicity, and making them presents, like the master of a family, ver. 43, and 2 Kings vi. 18. (Calmet) — Any superior may bless. (Worthington)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Oil. The precise signification of the Hebrew Ashisha, is not known. (Calmet) — Protestants, “a flagon of wine.” (Haydock) — It denotes any thing “exhilarating.” (Tirinus) (Du Hamel)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Appointed, as a prophet, (Haydock) or by dispensation. The high priest has supreme spiritual power, Deuteronomy xvii. 18., &c. (Worthington)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Zacharias, at the head of the second band. These played on the nable and kinnor, as the women did not sing in the tabernacle. Jahiel should be Oziel, (chap. xv. 18., &c.; Calmet) unless he be the same person, chap. xv. 18. (Haydock) — Instruments. Literally, “organs.” (Haydock) — Hebrew, “the instruments of nables.” (Vatable) (Menochius)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Jaziel. We read Eliezer, &c., chap. xv. 24. — Continually. At the appointed times."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Made. Hebrew, “gave first, to thank the Lord, into the hand of Asaph and his brethren this psalm,” (Haydock) in order the 104th; and from ver. 23 we find the 95th in the Psalter, with some improvements. (Tirinus)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Power. The ark was frequently the instrument of God’s power; and his people are exhorted to present themselves to pray before it. (Calmet) — God seemed to dwell there, and often displayed his wonders. (Du Hamel)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Israel. In Psalm civ., Abraham."],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Prophets. The ancient patriarchs ruled their families as kings, and directed them by word and example, like the most holy prophets. (Calmet) — Both are frequently styled anointed. (Haydock)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Place. In Psalm xcv. 6., holiness and majesty in his sanctuary. Hebrew, “strength and beauty in,” &c. God is served in his tabernacle with piety."],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","Sacrifice, of flour and wine. — Beauty. Hebrew, “in the beauty of his sanctuary.” Psalms, Come into his courts: adore ye the Lord in his holy court."],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Presence. Here the psalm adds, say ye among, as [in] ver. 31."],["haydock",35,null,"haydock","And say, &c., is not in the psalm; and it seems not to agree with the flourishing state of David’s kingdom: yet he might speak with the spirit of prophecy, or wish that all his subjects might live in happiness and concord under him. (Calmet) — Others think that Esdras inserted these verses to be sung in the second temple, (Lyranus; Estius) when many still remained in captivity. Yet they may refer to the time of Christ. (Du Hamel)"],["haydock",36,null,"haydock","And sing. Hebrew, “and Alleluia,” or, “all….said, (or shall say) truth and praise be to the Lord.”"],["haydock",39,null,"haydock","Sadoc was to minister at Gabaon, with Heman, Idithun, &c., while Abiathar, Asaph, &c., officiated at Jerusalem. (Calmet) — This regulation continued till towards the end of David’s reign, (Haydock) when fresh orders were given, chap. xxiii., &c. We find Abiathar and Sadoc were both at Jerusalem, when David was obliged to flee, 2 Kings xv. 24., and 3 Kings i. 7. The altar at Gabaon continued some time longer, 3 Kings iii. 3."],["haydock",43,null,"haydock","Bless, or feast, (Du Hamel) with his family, on part of the sacred victims. (Haydock)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}